  This ledger audits the 17 advertised theorem declarations of the imported
  OpenAI-YM source at revision <verbatim|3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464>.
  It is a statement and interpretation audit, not a proof of their
  mathematical content. The corresponding source-level signatures and check
  evidence are in <hlink|<verbatim|source/audit.md>|../source/audit.tm>. All
  source paths below are relative to the repository root. The public theorem
  is <verbatim|YangMills.jointExistence>; the other release headlines have
  distinct scopes and must not be read as extra conjuncts of that theorem.

  <section|Conventions and evidence><label|conventions-and-evidence>

  <verbatim|YangMills.GaugeGroup> is a closed matrix subgroup in a specified
  finite unitary representation. Its <verbatim|IsSimple> predicate refers to
  the associated real Lie algebra: it is nonabelian and has no proper Lie
  ideals. The internal <verbatim|YM.Gauge>/<verbatim|Family> notation is
  related by a bridge, but a theorem formulated in that notation does not
  thereby become a public Wilson-family theorem. All spacetime coordinates in
  the public challenge are four-dimensional; the time coordinate is
  distinguished. These are definitions of this formal source, not additional
  assumptions about every gauge theory.

  The <hlink|accepted issue #2 axiom evidence|../source/evidence/axioms-3456e5e.tm>
  reports <verbatim|propext>, <verbatim|Classical.choice>, and
  <verbatim|Quot.sound> for every root, with no nonstandard endpoint axiom.
  It used a checksum-verified compiled artifact and the pinned Lean 4.32.0
  kernel; it was not a clean build from source on this host. Those facts
  concern proof terms, not the physical interpretation of a statement.

  <strong|Assumption layers.> The theorem hypotheses are the displayed
  simplicity, positivity, geometry, filter, construction, and lower-grade
  conditions in the entries below. Existential witnesses such as a family,
  hierarchy, refinement, table, reader, or scaling are constructed data, not
  hypotheses silently imposed on all gauge theories. The formal proof
  environment uses the three reported Lean axioms and pinned Mathlib; neither
  the public theorem nor this ledger adds an external physical axiom such as
  a mass gap. No literature result is used to replace inspection of these
  formal definitions.

  <section|Public and assembly roots><label|public-and-assembly-roots>

  <subsection|<verbatim|YangMills.jointExistence>><label|yangmillsjointexistence>

  <strong|Source:> <verbatim|Lean/Solution.lean:4>, with the statement
  <verbatim|JointExistence> in <verbatim|Lean/ChallengeSpec.lean:186\U188>.
  <strong|Input:> every <verbatim|g : GaugeGroup> with <verbatim|g.IsSimple>.
  <strong|Conclusion:> there is one <verbatim|C : WilsonFamily g> satisfying
  <verbatim|WilsonRegularity C>. For <em|every> strictly increasing sequence
  <verbatim|p : \<bbb-N\> \<rightarrow\> \<bbb-N\>> of volumes, there is a
  strictly increasing <verbatim|q : \<bbb-N\> \<rightarrow\> \<bbb-N\>> and a
  <verbatim|T : Hierarchy C> such that <verbatim|T.Properties (p \<circ\> q)>
  holds. The order of these quantifiers permits the hierarchy and subsequence
  to depend on <verbatim|p>, while the Wilson family is chosen before
  <verbatim|p>.

  The meanings of <verbatim|WilsonRegularity> and
  <verbatim|Hierarchy.Properties> are fixed by
  <verbatim|Lean/ChallengeSpec.lean> and the public Wilson and theory
  definitions. The property record comprises Wilson convergence, a selected
  reconstructed theory, an all-orders separated operator-product expansion,
  and a short-distance nonfreeness test for a centered grade-four field. The
  reconstruction's spectral condition is positive energy and uniqueness of
  the invariant vacuum. Neither a positive mass gap nor uniqueness of the
  continuum limit appears as a conclusion. This is the principal public
  statement; the detailed construction below supplies its witnesses.

  More precisely, regularity supplies one positive running slope such that
  the ratio of the bare action coefficient to
  <verbatim|log(mesh\<#207B\>\<onesuperior\>)> approaches it uniformly over
  the volume index; continuity and gauge invariance of every insertion; and,
  for each insertion token, one finite lattice-offset stencil that controls
  its values at every spacing, volume and placement
  (<verbatim|ChallengeSpec.lean:11\U22>). <verbatim|C.Converges> first takes
  mesh limits of smeared finite Wilson moments on suitable volume rows, then
  a volume limit to the unselected hierarchy correlation
  (<verbatim|PublicWilson.lean:75\U90>). The four fields of
  <verbatim|Hierarchy.Properties> are exactly <verbatim|wilson>,
  <verbatim|reconstruction>, <verbatim|ope>, and <verbatim|nonfree>
  (<verbatim|ChallengeSpec.lean:177\U182>); contacts are not a fifth field.

  The reconstruction field includes faithful tempered fields on a common
  dense cyclic domain, nontriviality and label representation, a probability
  measure with full support on characters, permutation symmetry and
  smoothness of separated correlations, continuity in the character, and a
  phase-mixture identity. Its selected Wightman theory satisfies normalized
  vacuum, positive-energy spectrum in the <em|closed> forward cone,
  uniqueness of the translation-invariant vacuum up to scalar, Poincaré
  covariance, and spacelike locality. Analytic continuation on ordered
  complex times has the stated Euclidean root and Wightman boundary values on
  product Schwartz tests (<verbatim|ChallengeSpec.lean:117\U143>;
  <verbatim|PublicTheory.lean:252\U312>). These conditions do not require a
  spectral interval above the vacuum to be empty.

  The OPE has one phase-independent coefficient system. For fixed labels,
  geometry constants, truncation degree <verbatim|D>, and spectator
  separation, the remainder is bounded by
  <verbatim|r^(D+1-weight(a)-\<varepsilon\>)> at all sufficiently small
  positive <verbatim|r>, uniformly over admissible positions and characters.
  Coefficient bounds are stated separately for each degree and channel.
  <verbatim|Within> requires cluster points within <verbatim|Mr> of the
  center and separated by at least <verbatim|\<eta\>r>; <verbatim|Clear>
  requires spectators separated from the center and from each other by at
  least <verbatim|c> (<verbatim|ChallengeSpec.lean:53\U80>).
  Coefficient-bound thresholds may depend on degree and channel; a
  fixed-truncation remainder threshold is uniform over the channels in its
  finite sum but may depend on that truncation and the labels. No threshold
  uniform over all degrees, truncations, or label choices is asserted.

  The trace test centers a grade-four field and gives the selected two-point
  function along the time axis the positive limit <verbatim|r\<#2078\>
  log\<twosuperior\>(r\<#207B\>\<onesuperior\>) traceTwo(r) \<rightarrow\> 3
  dim\<bbb-R\>(g.lieAlgebra)/\<pi\>\<#2074\>> as <verbatim|r \<downarrow\>
  0>. It excludes eventual equality near zero with every <em|finite> sum of
  the specified mass-shell Wick profiles (<verbatim|ChallengeSpec.lean:145\U175>).
  The README's physical trace identification and Maxwell Fock countertests
  refer to the detailed construction statement, beyond this four-field public
  property record.

  <subsection|<verbatim|YangMills.constructed>><label|yangmillsconstructed>

  <strong|Source:> <verbatim|Lean/Construction.lean:15\U31>. <strong|Inputs:>
  <verbatim|g : GaugeGroup>, <verbatim|g.IsSimple>, and a real
  blocking-kernel penalty <verbatim|b \<gtr\> 0>. <strong|Conclusion:> a
  <verbatim|YM.Family g.core> with penalty exactly <verbatim|b>, the internal
  construction property <verbatim|Makes>, and public
  <verbatim|WilsonRegularity> for its Wilson projection. For every internal
  extraction order <verbatim|p>, a refinement <verbatim|q> gives a table
  <verbatim|S>, final object <verbatim|F>, all-phase reconstruction
  <verbatim|L>, OPE reader <verbatim|Q>, contact devices <verbatim|M>, and
  scaling <verbatim|X>, simultaneously satisfying the projected public
  hierarchy properties, ordered extraction, full-volume position conditions,
  calibration, the internal independent core, reconstruction, separated and
  nested OPE, trace exclusions, contact/basis conditions, and the first
  extension and four position-meter tests.

  <verbatim|Lean/WilsonBridge.lean:10\U17> turns the public matrix group into
  the internal gauge structure. <verbatim|Lean/YM/Nonperturbative/HeadlineSpec.lean:143\U155>
  defines the internal existence package used in the proof. The extra contact
  and position conditions belong to this detailed result and its selected
  witnesses. They are not additional conjuncts in
  <verbatim|YangMills.jointExistence>. The statement gives an extension under
  the constructed contact prescription; it does not claim uniqueness across
  prescriptions or raw cutoff convergence at collisions.

  <subsection|<verbatim|YM.Nonperturbative.existence>><label|ymnonperturbativeexistence>

  <strong|Source:> <verbatim|Lean/YM/Nonperturbative/Headlines.lean:19\U20>,
  expanded by <verbatim|Headline.WilsonExistence> in
  <verbatim|Lean/YM/Nonperturbative/HeadlineSpec.lean:143\U155>.
  <strong|Inputs:> an internal closed matrix gauge group <verbatim|G>, proof
  <verbatim|G.Simple>, and a positive penalty <verbatim|b>.
  <strong|Conclusion:> an internal family <verbatim|C> with that penalty and
  <verbatim|C.Makes>; for each extraction order, refined table/final data,
  every-phase almanac, OPE reader, contact devices, and scaling satisfying
  ordered extraction, full-volume position tests, independent core,
  reconstruction, separated and nested OPE, trace exclusions, basis identity,
  contact transport/basis/threshold conditions, and
  first-extension/four-meter conditions. The proof of <verbatim|constructed>
  invokes this existence result and projects it through the public bridges. A
  field or condition named in this internal package must be checked through
  its definition before assigning a physical interpretation.

  <subsection|<verbatim|YM.Nonperturbative.continuum_with_position_tests>><label|ymnonperturbativecontinuum_with_position_tests>

  <strong|Source:> <verbatim|Lean/YM/Nonperturbative/Continuum.lean:28\U39>.
  <strong|Inputs:> internal <verbatim|G.Simple> and <verbatim|b \<gtr\> 0>;
  the construction run <verbatim|R := fundedRun hG hb> is fixed.
  <strong|Conclusion:> <verbatim|R> has penalty <verbatim|b>, and for every
  extraction order <verbatim|p> there exist a refinement, table, final
  object, almanac, reader, devices, and scaling with ordered full extraction,
  full-volume positions, <verbatim|Independent.Core>, uniform nested OPE,
  equality of device and reader field bases, multiple contact properties,
  nonempty transport basis and thresholds, <verbatim|FirstExtension>, and
  <verbatim|FourMeters>. This theorem feeds
  <verbatim|YM.Nonperturbative.existence>; it is phrased in the internal run
  and table framework, before the public Wilson projection.

  <subsection|<verbatim|YM.Nonperturbative.continuum>><label|ymnonperturbativecontinuum>

  <strong|Source:> <verbatim|Lean/YM/Nonperturbative/Continuum.lean:52\U62>.
  <strong|Inputs:> the same internal <verbatim|G.Simple>, positive
  <verbatim|b>, and funded run. <strong|Conclusion:> the same penalty and
  refined table/final/almanac/reader/devices package as the preceding
  theorem, omitting the scaling, first-extension, and four-meter conjuncts.
  Its proof projects <verbatim|continuum_with_position_tests>; it is a weaker
  companion statement, not an additional premise of
  <verbatim|jointExistence>.

  <section|Concrete SU(2) roots><label|concrete-su2-roots>

  <subsection|<verbatim|YangMills.su2Simple>><label|yangmillssu2simple>

  <strong|Source:> <verbatim|Lean/ConcreteSU2.lean:12>. <strong|Conclusion:>
  the explicitly packaged two-dimensional unitary matrix group
  <verbatim|YangMills.su2> satisfies the source's
  <verbatim|GaugeGroup.IsSimple> predicate.
  <verbatim|Lean/ConcreteSU2.lean:8\U10> defines this group using
  <verbatim|YM.Next.Foundations.SUtwo> and the public gauge-group structure;
  <verbatim|Lean/PublicTheory.lean> supplies the simplicity meaning. This
  result supplies the hypothesis of <verbatim|su2Theory>; it asserts no
  Wilson construction on its own.

  <subsection|<verbatim|YangMills.su2Theory>><label|yangmillssu2theory>

  <strong|Source:> <verbatim|Lean/ConcreteSU2.lean:15\U18>.
  <strong|Conclusion:> for the concrete <verbatim|su2>, there exists a
  regular Wilson family such that every strictly increasing volume sequence
  has a strictly increasing subsequence and a hierarchy satisfying the public
  <verbatim|Properties> record. This is <verbatim|jointExistence su2
  su2Simple>, with no new physical conclusion. It does not assert a mass gap.

  <section|Focused companion roots><label|focused-companion-roots>

  <subsection|<verbatim|YM.Independent.Core.point>><label|ymindependentcorepoint>

  <strong|Source:> <verbatim|Lean/YM/Independent/Core.lean:49\U63>.
  <strong|Inputs:> an internal <verbatim|Core> for a final object, almanac,
  and OPE reader; finite sets of clustered and spectator labels, nonnegative
  cluster-size constant <verbatim|M>, positive room, separation, and loss
  constants <verbatim|\<eta\>,c,\<varepsilon\>>, and degree <verbatim|D>.
  <strong|Conclusion:> for all sufficiently small positive radii
  <verbatim|r>, uniformly over clustered positions <verbatim|x> in
  <verbatim|WithinRoom r M \<eta\>> about a center <verbatim|o>, spectator
  positions <verbatim|y> in <verbatim|ClearOf o y c>, and viewers
  <verbatim|s>, the norm of the whole correlation minus the finite OPE sum
  over degrees <verbatim|j \<leq\> D> and reader channels is at most
  <verbatim|r^(D+1-weight(d)-\<varepsilon\>)>. The sum uses the same table's
  spectator correlation with one field inserted at <verbatim|o>.
  <verbatim|Lean/YM/OPE/Orbits/Profile.lean> defines the geometry and
  exponent; <verbatim|Lean/YM/OPE/Orbits/Words.lean> defines the label words.
  The theorem extracts a consequence of an assumed <verbatim|Core>, rather
  than constructing a public hierarchy. A pinned constant-closure query finds
  this declaration reachable from <verbatim|jointExistence>'s type or proof;
  its pointwise estimate is still not a separate conjunct of the public
  <verbatim|Properties> record.

  <subsection|<verbatim|YM.Contacts.Solstice.celestial>><label|ymcontactssolsticecelestial>

  <strong|Source:> <verbatim|Lean/YM/Contacts/Solstice/Result.lean:78\U79>.
  <strong|Input:> an internal <verbatim|Gauge G> with <verbatim|G.Simple>.
  <strong|Conclusion:> there exist an internal family, extraction order,
  table, final object, and contact devices satisfying
  <verbatim|MultipleLight>, with a nonempty transport system basis and
  nonempty threshold package. <verbatim|MultipleLight> is a record of
  services, a rotation identity, and conditional slope, moment, and
  distributional estimates under its own finite-dimensional Euclidean and
  contact-geometry hypotheses (<verbatim|Result.lean:52\U63>). These
  conditions are not a claim of uniqueness of contact extensions, reflection
  positivity on overlapping supports, arbitrary contact Ward identities, or
  real-time time-ordered products. This is a separately advertised existence
  statement; the issue #3 dependency audit does not identify it as a premise
  of <verbatim|jointExistence>.

  The conditional clauses quantify over finite-dimensional real inner-product
  test spaces equipped with the specified Haar/Lebesgue measure structure.
  The <verbatim|slope> field requires a rooted normal-group route, a nonempty
  group, <verbatim|Run>, <verbatim|Given>, route authority, and an eligible
  backdrop, and concludes the named <verbatim|Sunshine> estimate. The
  <verbatim|moment> field concludes <verbatim|Resolution> for a route and
  eligible backdrop under <verbatim|Given> and authority. The
  <verbatim|distribution> field concludes a <verbatim|Charge> bound for the
  major kernel when the contact plane lies in the test-space range, the
  configuration is collapsed, and <verbatim|0 \<less\> B \<leq\> 1>,
  <verbatim|B> is below the device clearance, and <verbatim|0 \<less\> s>
  with <verbatim|2s \<leq\> B>. These are the immediate formal conditions of
  <verbatim|MultipleLight>; they should not be replaced with an unrestricted
  contact-distribution claim.

  <verbatim|Sunshine> supplies power-law <verbatim|Claim> estimates for the
  backdrop, each root coefficient, and a remainder; for configurations with
  more than one point it also supplies conditional annulus estimates
  (<verbatim|Result.lean:33\U40>). <verbatim|Resolution> packages an
  <verbatim|Achieves> bound, a finite-part/series formula for the route
  integral, and integrability, absolute summability and <verbatim|HasSum>
  statements for the selected and centered contact expansions
  (<verbatim|Result.lean:23\U31>). <verbatim|Charge> is a compact-local
  test-order bound for the major-kernel distribution: on each compact test
  support, a constant controls the distribution by the prescribed jet order
  (<verbatim|Solstice/Venue.lean:30\U31>). Thus the existential root has
  contact estimates for this constructed prescription under the displayed
  geometry and eligibility conditions, not an arbitrary contact theory.

  <subsection|<verbatim|YM.Pert.Asymptotics.comparison>><label|ympertasymptoticscomparison>

  <strong|Source:> <verbatim|Lean/YM/Pert/Asymptotics/RawAxis.lean:36>.
  <strong|Inputs:> an internal family <verbatim|C> satisfying
  <verbatim|C.Makes>, a simple internal gauge group, arbitrary integer
  sequences <verbatim|N,k>, and the eventual condition <verbatim|i \<leq\>
  N(i)>. <strong|Conclusion:> as <verbatim|i \<rightarrow\> \<infty\>>, the
  running inverse square root of the bare action coefficient at
  <verbatim|(N(i),k(i),i)>, multiplied by the square root of the logarithm of
  the inverse spacing, converges to <verbatim|(\<sqrt\>(2
  b\<#2080\>(G)))\<#207B\>\<onesuperior\>>, where <verbatim|b\<#2080\>(G)=11
  casimir(G)/(48\<pi\>\<twosuperior\>)> (<verbatim|RawAxis.lean:13\U19>). The
  statement concerns the defined <verbatim|regulatorRun> proxy. It does not
  identify this quantity with an X-scheme physical coupling or establish an
  all-orders matching formula. Its relation to the public theorem is a
  focused companion assertion, not an extra public property.

  <subsection|<verbatim|YM.Pert.Position.finite_leading>><label|ympertpositionfinite_leading>

  <strong|Source:> <verbatim|Lean/YM/Pert/Position/Crest.lean:13>.
  <strong|Inputs:> a position condition <verbatim|Q> on a finite frame, its
  <verbatim|CleanProfiles> witness, and a lattice side <verbatim|L> with
  <verbatim|L.val \<gtr\> 1>. <strong|Conclusion:> the order-zero
  finite-regulator position ledger <verbatim|finite Q L m 0> tends, as
  <verbatim|m \<rightarrow\> \<infty\>>, to <verbatim|white Q> in its
  function/product topology. <verbatim|Conditions> in
  <verbatim|Position/Board.lean:54\U59> specifies slots, arities,
  field-valued columns, compact-test profiles and a linear chart;
  <verbatim|CleanProfiles> identifies the profiles with compactly supported
  smooth tests. The theorem fixes the side and concerns the leading
  coefficient only. It does not identify higher coefficients with continuum
  correlations or diagrams. A pinned constant-closure query finds this
  declaration reachable in the proof/type closure of
  <verbatim|jointExistence>, without changing the public theorem's
  conclusion.

  <subsection|<verbatim|YM.Pert.Position.normalized_four>><label|ympertpositionnormalized_four>

  <strong|Source:> <verbatim|Lean/YM/Pert/Position/Four.lean:56>.
  <strong|Inputs:> internal <verbatim|G.Simple> and a positive penalty
  <verbatim|p>. <strong|Conclusion:> an internal family with penalty
  <verbatim|p>, the named construction/material/plenty conditions, and, for
  each extraction order <verbatim|f>, a refinement, table, final object
  <verbatim|b> and scaling <verbatim|X> with <verbatim|FirstExtension b X>
  and <verbatim|FourMeters b X>. The latter requires a position
  <verbatim|Crest> and one degree-four, square, clean-profile condition whose
  board ledger is attained and resident along the same scaling
  (<verbatim|Four.lean:45\U47>). <verbatim|Scaling> contains spin changes and
  a positive scale decreasing to zero. The source describes ordinary unmarked
  correlations of the constructed mean theory along a weak sequence; the
  theorem does not assert all-order actual-versus-diagram matching.

  The named existence conditions are substantive. <verbatim|Wcmain> is a
  centered-budget bound at every grade, positive radius and loss, uniform
  over bounded reference vectors and auxiliary volume indices after a
  sufficiently large cutoff (<verbatim|Extraction/Center.lean:38\U40>).
  <verbatim|Materials> includes <verbatim|G.Simple>, <verbatim|C.Makes>,
  created/open-stock data and a paid room package
  (<verbatim|Pert/Spatial/Prices.lean:16\U22>). <verbatim|Plenty> requires at
  every schedule index that square-root <verbatim|hatBeta> dominate the
  support-exponent and fourth-power spacing-factor cost
  (<verbatim|Pert/Spatial/CrowdPay.lean:26\U30>). None of these names may be
  read as an all-orders perturbative matching assertion.

  <subsection|<verbatim|YM.Pert.Parity.parityEndpoint>><label|ympertparityparityendpoint>

  <strong|Source:> <verbatim|Lean/YM/Pert/Parity/End.lean:21>.
  <strong|Inputs:> internal <verbatim|G.Simple> and a positive penalty
  <verbatim|c>. <strong|Conclusion:> an internal family with that penalty and
  the named construction/material/plenty conditions; for every extraction
  order a refinement, table, final object and scaling with
  <verbatim|FirstExtension>, <verbatim|Crest>, and a clean-profile position
  condition whose ledger is attained and resident, together with
  <verbatim|Fair Q J> and <verbatim|ParityFields \<varphi\>>. <verbatim|Fair>
  is an exact algebraic mirror identity and center fixed-point equation
  (<verbatim|Parity/Branch.lean:17>). <verbatim|ParityFields> says that, for
  every target condition <verbatim|R>, side greater than one, and jet indices
  <verbatim|p,j>, an identity between the <verbatim|R> read jet and the
  coat-transformed condition's read jet holds eventually in the finite
  regulator, with factor <verbatim|(-1)^j>
  (<verbatim|Parity/End.lean:15\U17>). This is an eventual finite-regulator
  relation, not an unrestricted continuum parity theorem.

  <subsection|<verbatim|YM.Pert.Parity.low_read>><label|ympertparitylow_read>

  <strong|Source:> <verbatim|Lean/YM/Pert/Parity/EvenLow.lean:44>.
  <strong|Inputs:> a simple internal gauge group, a position condition
  <verbatim|X> on a frame, arity/grade <verbatim|d \<leq\> 5>, any lattice
  side and regulator, and an odd coefficient index <verbatim|j>.
  <strong|Conclusion:> the finite coefficient <verbatim|finite X L m j> is
  exactly zero in its ledger. No clean-profile or determinant hypothesis
  appears. The result is restricted to these low grades and finite
  coefficients; it does not itself state a continuum response or all-orders
  comparison.

  <subsection|<verbatim|YM.SharpGram.habitat>><label|ymsharpgramhabitat>

  <strong|Source:> <verbatim|Lean/YM/SharpGram/Measured.lean:11>.
  <strong|Inputs:> internal <verbatim|G.Simple>, a positive penalty
  <verbatim|b>, and an extraction order <verbatim|f>. <strong|Conclusion:>
  there exist an internal family, refinement, table, final object,
  every-phase almanac, and prescription with the stipulated construction,
  calibration, penalty, UV, order, margin, facility, logarithmic, completion,
  all-shapes, and dense-coverage conditions. Its final quantified clause runs
  over every ultrafilter below the fused extraction filter and every horizon
  <verbatim|D>: a weak portfolio exists, and each such portfolio has priced
  spin-change data whose basis readings converge grade by grade to the model.
  The full conjunction is transcribed in the source audit. This is a
  fixed-field and weak portfolio habitat; the statement does not assert a
  Gram matrix is positive definite in every physical regime or establish a
  mass gap.

  Here <verbatim|Floral> is a structured ultraviolet extension/cellar package
  with analytic arc, spring, geometric and eventual realization clauses
  (<verbatim|UVExtension/Oasis/Flora.lean:55\U72>); its many nested
  predicates are not collapsed to a standard continuum-limit theorem.
  <verbatim|Facilities> combines a same-table OPE service package with a
  logarithmic package (<verbatim|OPE/ElementaryLog.lean:12\U13>);
  <verbatim|AllShapes> adds coherent one-family, nested-tree, and discounted
  two-family subpower estimates (<verbatim|OPE/Subpower.lean:37\U45>).
  <verbatim|Dense.Coverage> chooses a common predecessor schedule and panels
  with eventual <verbatim|Poised> data at every horizon
  (<verbatim|SharpGram/Dense/Passes.lean:16\U24>). A weak
  <verbatim|Portfolio> carries binding, budget, chart and panel data, a late
  predecessor, matrix/field sequences and subfilter limits
  (<verbatim|SharpGram/Weak/Reading.lean:35\U49>); its nonemptiness is not
  the existence of a standalone probability measure.

  <subsection|<verbatim|YM.SharpGram.inletFirst>><label|ymsharpgraminletfirst>

  <strong|Source:> <verbatim|Lean/YM/SharpGram/Clauses.lean:126>.
  <strong|Inputs:> an internal simple gauge group, a family and final object,
  a filter <verbatim|\<varphi\>> on <verbatim|\<bbb-N\> \<times\> \<bbb-N\>>
  below the extraction order's fused filter, and a label <verbatim|x> of
  grade <verbatim|d \<leq\> 3>, and the remaining section data.
  <strong|Conclusion:> the projected field <verbatim|S.project d x> satisfies
  <verbatim|Supplies C \<varphi\> d>. Precisely, an exponent <verbatim|p> can
  be chosen so that, for every sufficiently large <verbatim|k>, there are a
  label <verbatim|y> projecting to that field and a label net <verbatim|q>
  tending to <verbatim|y> along <verbatim|\<varphi\>>, whose quota is
  eventually bounded by <verbatim|hatBeta(k)^p>
  (<verbatim|Clauses.lean:18\U21>). The statement concerns supply for grades
  through three under these hypotheses; it makes no higher-grade or
  public-hierarchy claim.

  <subsection|<verbatim|YM.SharpGram.outletFirst>><label|ymsharpgramoutletfirst>

  <strong|Source:> <verbatim|Lean/YM/SharpGram/Clauses.lean:120>.
  <strong|Inputs:> the same internal simple-group, family, final/table/ticket
  and filter context; a complex-linear functional <verbatim|L> on grade
  <verbatim|d>; a lower grade <verbatim|w \<leq\> d> with <verbatim|w \<leq\>
  3>; and <verbatim|Kills> on all grades below <verbatim|w>.
  <strong|Conclusion:> <verbatim|Releases C \<varphi\> d w L>. An exponent
  <verbatim|p> exists such that, for every sufficiently large <verbatim|k>,
  every nonbottom subfilter <verbatim|u \<leq\> \<varphi\>>, convergent label
  net <verbatim|q \<rightarrow\> x> along <verbatim|u>, and eventual quota
  bound <verbatim|z>, the value of <verbatim|L> on the projected field is
  bounded by <verbatim|hatBeta(k)^p z / a(k)^(d-w)>
  (<verbatim|Clauses.lean:23\U29>). The order of the eventual and universal
  quantifiers matters. It is an estimate conditional on annihilation of lower
  grades, not a general regularity theorem.

  <section|Interpretation limits and public
  prose><label|interpretation-limits-and-public-prose>

  The upstream <verbatim|Lean/README.md> describes Wilson origin,
  reconstructed Wightman properties, separated OPE, and a nonfreeness test
  for the public challenge. Those descriptions must be read with the actual
  predicates in <verbatim|ChallengeSpec.lean>, <verbatim|PublicWilson.lean>,
  and <verbatim|PublicTheory.lean>; they do not add hypotheses or conclusions
  to <verbatim|JointExistence>. The README explicitly excludes a positive
  mass gap, general exclusion of continuous-mass generalized free fields, and
  all-orders MS-bar matching. It also says the constructed contact
  prescription is an extension, without uniqueness over other prescriptions
  or raw cutoff convergence on collision diagonals.

  The release-headline list alone does not say which focused results occur in
  the proof term of <verbatim|YangMills.jointExistence>. The direct source
  chain inspected here is <verbatim|continuum_with_position_tests
  \<rightarrow\> existence \<rightarrow\> constructed \<rightarrow\>
  jointExistence>; <verbatim|continuum> is a projection of the first, and
  <verbatim|su2Theory> specializes the last. A pinned type/proof
  constant-closure query additionally finds
  <verbatim|YM.Independent.Core.point> and
  <verbatim|YM.Pert.Position.finite_leading> reachable from
  <verbatim|jointExistence>; the other focused headline names are not in that
  closure. Reachability records a formal dependency, not a public conjunct or
  a physical interpretation. The contact, parity, asymptotics and Sharp-Gram
  headlines remain separately advertised statements.
</body>

